WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.500
[CLICKING]

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.470
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:02.450
[RUSTLING]

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:06.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:11.070
PROFESSOR: Why is it so hard for
some people to recognize faces?

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.800
Is a pterodactyl
really a dinosaur, and

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.530
what does that have to do
with ethics in computing?

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.920
Are GMOs helpful or
harmful, and how do we know?

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.350
Is calculus a language?

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.140
Welcome to Chalk
Radio season three.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.870
This season, we'll talk
with more MIT professors

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.480
about the passions that fuel
their cutting edge research

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.430
and innovative
teaching, of topics

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.560
like neurocognitive
science, climate,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.990
laboratory chemistry, computer
science, and many more.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:40.650
So tune in to hear
candid, funny, serious,

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.790
and personal conversations.

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.370
And as always we'll share
links in our show notes

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:49.080
so you can find our guests' open
educational resources on MIT

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.550
OpenCourseWare.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.920
Chalk Radio, it's the
closest thing to being here.

